class NotesEdit(BaseModel):
    """
    Replace ONE exact span of a notes document -- the Edit-tool contract.

    `old_text` must appear EXACTLY ONCE in the stored document. Zero matches or two
    is a 409 naming the count, never a guess: an agent that retries with more
    surrounding context is doing the right thing, and an edit applied to the wrong
    one of two identical spans is a silent corruption. `new_text = ""` deletes the
    matched span.

    WHY THIS IS SAFE UNDER CONCURRENCY, without a version column. The match runs
    inside the same UPDATE that writes, against the row's OWN column, so a writer
    blocked on the row lock re-evaluates against the winner's committed document --
    the same property that makes `notes_append` safe. If the other writer changed
    the span you targeted, your `old_text` no longer matches and you get a 409
    instead of overwriting their edit. If they changed a DIFFERENT span, your edit
    still lands. That is per-span optimistic concurrency with nothing for the caller
    to track, which is why agents need no version bookkeeping anywhere in notes.

    Matching is plain exact substring comparison over the stored text: NO Unicode
    normalization, NO CRLF folding. The document is exactly the bytes a reader GETs,
    so an agent that copies a span out of a read can always match it back; a
    normalizing matcher would accept text that is not in the document and silently
    rewrite something adjacent.

    A retry after a timeout SELF-HEALS: the first attempt removed `old_text`, so the
    replay finds zero matches and 409s -- the caller re-reads and sees its edit
    already landed. That is why there is no idempotency key here.
    """

    new_text: str = Field(..., max_length=100000, title='New Text')
    old_text: str = Field(..., max_length=100000, min_length=1, title='Old Text')


class ObservedClientOut(BaseModel):
    """
    One client we have actually heard from, stale or not.

    THE POINT OF THIS MODEL IS THAT IT IS NOT FILTERED. `clients` below carries
    only what is wrong, which is why an empty response has always been ambiguous:
    it means "everything is current" and "we have never heard from you" equally,
    and a reader cannot tell which. This list separates those -- an entry proves
    a report arrived, and `last_seen_at` says when.

    `status` is None when the client is at or above `latest`. It is deliberately
    the ONLY judgment here: we report the version and the moment it was reported,
    and never infer anything about the machine's updater from it. A version that
    has not moved may mean a broken updater, an unused machine, or simply that no
    newer release exists -- the observation cannot tell those apart, so it does
    not try.
    """

    current: str = Field(..., title='Current')
    kind: ClientKind
    last_seen_at: AwareDatetime = Field(..., title='Last Seen At')
    latest: str | None = Field(None, title='Latest')
    min: str | None = Field(None, title='Min')
    status: ClientUpdateStatus | None = None


