class RunReopenReceipt(BaseModel):
    """
    What a reopen hands back, so the client can resume WITHOUT re-reading.

    `last_step` arms the SDK's monotonic-step guard: a relaunch that continues
    from a checkpoint must not re-log steps the first execution already wrote,
    and it can only know where to start from here.

    The HARDWARE rail is excluded from that maximum on purpose. Its step index
    is `floor(unix_time/60)` (~29.7M in 2026), orders of magnitude above any
    training step, so including it would arm the guard above every step the
    relaunch is about to write and refuse the whole run. The SDK carries a
    matching client-side floor (`SUSPECT_RESUME_FLOOR`) that detects a server
    predating this exclusion and warns instead of arming.

    `write_epoch` counts how many times this run has been opened -- 1 for its
    original execution, 2 on the first reopen. Since 0185 it FENCES: telemetry
    writes carrying an older epoch are refused at the door, so a zombie
    predecessor still logging cannot interleave with the relaunch. Carry it on
    every write from the handle this receipt opens.

    `rewound_to` echoes the honored `rewind_to_step`, and the SDK REQUIRES the
    echo when it asked for one: a backend predating rewind ignores the unknown
    body field and reopens without deleting anything, and the missing echo is
    how that skew is told apart from success. `deleted_points`/`deleted_spans`
    say what the rewind actually removed (0 on an idempotent replay -- the
    first pass already removed them).
    """

    deleted_points: int | None = Field(None, title='Deleted Points')
    deleted_spans: int | None = Field(None, title='Deleted Spans')
    last_step: int | None = Field(None, title='Last Step')
    rewound_to: int | None = Field(None, title='Rewound To')
    run: RunDetailOut
    write_epoch: int = Field(..., title='Write Epoch')


